maya: Quick FYI — I audited the Troubleshooting & Error Reference pages and found ~18 places where examples still reference the old rate-limit header and the legacy JS snippet that uses .on('rateLimit'). We shipped the new signal names last quarter and docs are inconsistent. I'm going to open a small PR to: update SDK snippets (JS + Python), add a migration note, and fix a couple broken internal links.

sam: SGTM. can you also fold in a short section explaining when to treat 429 vs timeouts as transient vs terminal? customers keep confusing context-trim vs retry. a 1-2 bullet matrix would help. :+1:

maya: yep — planning a tiny matrix. will add recommended backoff + context-trim order of operations. proposed JS backoff snippet (will include in PR):

```js
// simple exponential backoff + retry for 429/timeouts
async function callWithBackoff(fn, maxRetries = 5) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (true) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      const isTransient = err.status === 429 || err.code === 'ETIMEDOUT';
      if (!isTransient || attempt >= maxRetries) throw err;
      const delay = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempt, 10000);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, delay));
      attempt++;
    }
  }
}
```

eli: Nice. can we also call out header guidance for clients behind proxies? some customers see 429s because proxies rewrite headers and lose the retry-after. add a short note about trusting server Retry-After vs client-side heuristics.

maya: good catch — will add a troubleshooting tip and example curl invocation showing the Retry-After header:

```bash
curl -I -s -D - https://api.redwood.ai/generate | grep -i 'Retry-After' || echo 'no retry-after header'
```

